1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,549
When an editor publishes an article or a visitor registers, something else often has to happen.

2
00:00:06,549 --> 00:00:11,329
The Workflows module lets you describe that follow-up as a diagram, with no code.

3
00:00:11,329 --> 00:00:14,680
In this video, we'll build a workflow and watch it run.

4
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:22,724
Sites often need to react to what happens on them:

5
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:26,968
an article is published, a user registers, a form is submitted.

6
00:00:26,968 --> 00:00:31,520
Doing it by hand is slow, and writing code for every case is costly.

7
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:35,708
With the Workflows module, you draw the reaction in the admin.

8
00:00:35,708 --> 00:00:39,551
Orchard Core runs the diagram each time the event happens, and you can

9
00:00:39,551 --> 00:00:41,270
inspect the runs afterwards.

10
00:00:45,470 --> 00:00:47,985
A workflow is a diagram of activities.

11
00:00:47,985 --> 00:00:53,587
An event is an activity that waits for something to happen, such as content being published.

12
00:00:53,587 --> 00:00:55,930
Only an event can start a workflow.

13
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:01,785
A task is an activity that does something, such as showing a notification,

14
00:01:01,785 --> 00:01:04,720
sending an email, or publishing content.

15
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:10,095
Each activity has outcomes, which are the exits it can take, such as Done.

16
00:01:10,095 --> 00:01:15,530
You connect an outcome to the next activity, and that connection is called a transition.

17
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:22,741
When the event happens, Orchard Core starts an instance of the workflow and follows the transitions.

18
00:01:22,741 --> 00:01:27,800
An instance can finish at once, or wait for another event and continue later.

19
00:01:32,005 --> 00:01:37,406
Orchard Core turns capabilities on as features, and Workflows is one of them.

20
00:01:37,406 --> 00:01:41,245
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for workflows.

21
00:01:41,698 --> 00:01:44,753
Workflows is the feature the others build on.

22
00:01:44,753 --> 00:01:51,402
HTTP Workflows Activities, Session Workflows Activities, and Timer Workflows Activities add activities of

23
00:01:51,402 --> 00:01:54,278
their own, and each is enabled separately.

24
00:01:54,732 --> 00:01:56,539
Let's enable Workflows.

25
00:01:56,539 --> 00:02:02,582
The Contents module provides the content events this video uses, once Workflows is enabled.

26
00:02:06,789 --> 00:02:09,849
Workflows is under Design in the admin menu.

27
00:02:09,849 --> 00:02:12,052
The page lists your workflows.

28
00:02:12,052 --> 00:02:14,929
This site has none, so the list is empty.

29
00:02:15,416 --> 00:02:17,394
Let's select Create Workflow.

30
00:02:17,394 --> 00:02:21,123
It asks only for a name, and the name says what the workflow

31
00:02:21,123 --> 00:02:25,136
does, such as notifying the editors when an article is published.

32
00:02:25,627 --> 00:02:27,282
Let's open More settings.

33
00:02:27,282 --> 00:02:31,337
It holds options, such as whether the workflow is enabled and how many

34
00:02:31,337 --> 00:02:32,938
instances run at a time.

35
00:02:32,938 --> 00:02:36,407
The defaults suit most workflows, so they stay as they are.

36
00:02:36,878 --> 00:02:39,518
Let's select Create and open the designer.

37
00:02:39,518 --> 00:02:43,478
It saves the workflow and opens the designer with an empty canvas.

38
00:02:47,697 --> 00:02:51,308
The toolbar shows the name of the workflow, with a link back to

39
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:54,397
Workflows, and the Publish and Discard draft buttons.

40
00:02:54,853 --> 00:03:00,102
The Activities pane on the left lists everything you can add, grouped by category.

41
00:03:00,102 --> 00:03:03,323
Search it by name, or show only Events or Tasks.

42
00:03:03,815 --> 00:03:07,782
The canvas in the middle is where the workflow takes shape.

43
00:03:07,782 --> 00:03:13,275
A workflow runs when something happens, so an empty one offers events to start with.

44
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:17,007
On the right, the workflow panel has three tabs.

45
00:03:17,007 --> 00:03:22,343
Variables holds named values, Workflow holds settings such as the name, and Issues

46
00:03:22,343 --> 00:03:24,223
lists the problems found.

47
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:32,118
Under Start with an event, let's pick the Content Published Event.

48
00:03:32,118 --> 00:03:36,780
The first event you add becomes the start activity, and its card shows a Start badge.

49
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:40,225
Its settings open at the bottom of the canvas.

50
00:03:40,225 --> 00:03:43,703
Content Types limits the event to the types you choose.

51
00:03:43,703 --> 00:03:47,750
Let's pick Article, so other content does not start the workflow.

52
00:03:48,232 --> 00:03:50,982
Each activity shows its outcomes as ports.

53
00:03:50,982 --> 00:03:55,165
Let's point at the Done port, where a plus sign appears, then click

54
00:03:55,165 --> 00:03:56,942
it and search for Notify.

55
00:03:57,403 --> 00:03:59,195
Let's select Notify Task.

56
00:03:59,195 --> 00:04:03,243
It is added to the right, connected to Done by a transition, and

57
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:05,903
it displays a notification when it runs.

58
00:04:06,354 --> 00:04:10,643
A message is required, so the card and the Issues tab flag a problem.

59
00:04:10,643 --> 00:04:14,704
Let's type a message and leave the field, and the problem clears.

60
00:04:15,159 --> 00:04:18,940
The designer saves your changes as a draft while you work, and a

61
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:20,290
draft does not run.

62
00:04:20,290 --> 00:04:24,179
Let's select Publish, which makes the draft the version that runs.

63
00:04:28,388 --> 00:04:29,734
Let's trigger it.

64
00:04:29,734 --> 00:04:35,588
Under Content, Content Items, Edit opens an article in the editor, which has a Publish button.

65
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:37,595
Let's select Publish.

66
00:04:37,595 --> 00:04:41,970
That makes the Content Published Event happen, so the workflow starts.

67
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:46,748
The Notify Task shows its message, next to the notification Orchard Core adds

68
00:04:46,748 --> 00:04:48,590
when content is published.

69
00:04:52,816 --> 00:04:57,762
Back in Design, Workflows, the workflow has an Instances badge, because each run

70
00:04:57,762 --> 00:05:00,236
of a workflow is kept as an instance.

71
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:04,638
The page counts the instances by status, and this one is Finished.

72
00:05:04,638 --> 00:05:08,970
Let's open it to see the activities that ran, highlighted on the diagram.

73
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:17,825
HTTP Workflows Activities receive requests and call web services.

74
00:05:17,825 --> 00:05:21,169
Timer Workflows Activities run on a schedule.

75
00:05:21,169 --> 00:05:24,710
Other modules add events and tasks of their own.

76
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:29,623
A workflow can branch with an If Else Task, repeat with loops, and

77
00:05:29,623 --> 00:05:31,415
keep values in variables.

78
00:05:31,415 --> 00:05:33,960
A separate video covers them in depth.

79
00:05:34,710 --> 00:05:36,762
Permissions guard workflows.

80
00:05:36,762 --> 00:05:42,798
Manage workflows is needed to build them, and Execute workflows to run them from the designer.

81
00:05:42,798 --> 00:05:46,420
By default, only administrators have Manage workflows.

82
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:52,518
Workflows let a site react to what happens on it, with activities you

83
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:53,939
connect on a canvas.

84
00:05:53,939 --> 00:05:55,360
Thanks for watching.

